{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794532660188,"sessionID":"ses_e2d8507c1ffeeKHr8YUEWWSQ84","part":{"partID":"prt_1d27b51760012TdeKkquRXn1cs","sessionID":"ses_e2d8507c1ffeeKHr8YUEWWSQ84","messageID":"msg_1d27b4815001QVjpAYWv9k1Cwx","type":"tool","id":"call_41fac7dbdd724809b0c13b07","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md","offset":28,"limit":40},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md, lines 28-67\n28: \n29: ---\n30: \n31: ## 2. WHAT IT DOES\n32: \n33: On each user-prompt event the adapter:\n34: \n35: 1. **Kill-switch.** Checks `isHookEnabled('skill-advisor')` (master `SYSTEM_HOOKS_DISABLED` or the `skill-advisor` family). Disabled → empty/skipped prompt-safe output.\n36: 2. **Read the prompt + workspace.** Resolves the workspace root (install-anchored walk, not CWD-relative, so the hook stays correct off-root), reads the prompt text (bounded), and resolves the session id.\n37: 3. **Prompt gate.** `skippedAdvisorResultFor` uses `shouldFireAdvisor` to decline skip commands, short acknowledgements and prompts below its length threshold. A declined prompt returns a skipped result and does not spawn the CLI.\n38: 4. **CLI front door.** The hook calls `buildSkillAdvisorBriefFromCli`, which runs `skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json - --no-warm-only` within the `SPECKIT_CLAUDE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS` budget (2,500 ms by default). It writes the request to the CLI's stdin, so the prompt never appears in the process table. The request carries at most the prompt's first 10,000 characters, the most `advisor_recommend` accepts, so a longer prompt is routed on its head instead of failing open. The Claude shim sets the child budget to 2,200 ms when the operator has not set the variable. An operator value above about 2,200 ms lets the shim kill the hook at 2,500 ms first, so the turn gets `{}`. The CLI owns the warm-daemon probe, starts the daemon when the socket is cold and falls back to the local Python scorer with a `degraded` result. The request carries `includeCompiledRoute: true`, because the advisor derives a recommendation's slash command from the compiled route. It also carries `compiledRouteTopOnly: true`, because each route lookup is a subprocess inside the hook's budget and the brief leads with the top pick. The hook keeps the `command` field and reads nothing from the route itself. A daemon still running an older build rejects these options with JSON-RPC `-32602`, and the CLI retries once without them.\n39: 5. **Render.** `renderAdvisorBrief` produces the model-visible `Advisor:` text. When there is no brief, the handler emits `renderAdvisorFallbackDirective`: one status line, then the directives block. The line reads `Advisor: outage (<status>)` with the command to route by hand when the advisor failed or timed out, `Advisor: no skill matched.` when it answered with nothing above threshold, and `Advisor: prompt skipped.` when the prompt gate declined. The `/doctor:setup` hint appears on no-match and outage lines for prompts that use set up, set-up or setup wording, install or reinstall wording including installation and installer, configure or configuration wording or `check` followed within three words by environment, env, config, hooks, tools, tooling, toolchain or skilled. The no-match line ends with ` For setup or health checks, run /doctor:setup.` The outage line places `for setup or health checks, run /doctor:setup;` before the hand-route command so it stays last. Raw prompt text is never persisted in diagnostics, cache metadata, status, or attribution.\n40: 6. **Directive-lifecycle dedup.** `decideDirectiveLifecycleDelivery` suppresses a re-delivery of the same directive to the same session within its cadence, so a repeated prompt does not re-inject an identical brief. A repeat in a known session keeps the first line, a route or a fallback status, and drops the directives block.\n41: 7. **Emit.** Native adapters emit a `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext` JSON envelope. OpenCode appends to `output.system` via `experimental.chat.system.transform`. Pi sends a model-visible message.\n42: \n43: The injected brief begins with `Advisor:` and names the routed skill or, when the recommendation resolves to a command, the skill that owns it and the runnable command. A doctor command appears as its runnable `/doctor:` string on the top line or under a skill as an `Also:` second line. The handler returns `{}` when the hook is disabled, input cannot be parsed or an unhandled exception occurs. A shim also returns `{}` when it cannot resolve or relay valid child output, including on timeout. When the handler has no brief, it emits the directives fallback.\n44: \n45: ### Brief head form\n46: \n47: A recommendation that resolves to a command renders one label, shared by the `use` and `ambiguous` heads:\n48: \n49: ```text\n50: <ownerSkillId or skill> → <command> (<policy>)\n51: ```\n52: \n53: The recommendation's own `ownerSkillId`, `command` and `policy` are authoritative, and the generated command row fills only what they omit. The policy parenthesis is dropped when no policy is known. A recommendation with no command row keeps its skill label rather than a command label.\n54: \n55: The `use` head reads `Advisor: <freshness>; use <label> <confidence>/<uncertainty> pass.` For example:\n56: \n57: ```text\n58: Advisor: live; use system-spec-kit → /speckit:save (start) 0.89/0.12 pass.\n59: ```\n60: \n61: The ambiguous head applies the same label to both sides: `Advisor: <freshness>; ambiguous: <label> <confidence>/<uncertainty> vs <label> <confidence>/<uncertainty> pass.` Each label part passes `sanitizeSkillLabel`, so a control character, a line break or an instruction-shaped label renders nothing rather than reaching the model. The `Also:` line still names a doctor command by its runnable string.\n62: \n63: ### Degraded contract\n64: \n65: When the daemon is unreachable, the CLI runs the local Python scorer and returns `status: ok` with `degraded: true` and `source: local-scorer`. The local scorer returns command row ids, which a reader cannot type, so the CLI rewrites each row before the brief renders:\n66: \n67: - A row whose bare slash command appears in the prompt (the row's `command` text before the first space) keeps its command fields: `skillId` is the row id, `kind` is `command`, `command` is the runnable string, `policy` is present when the row has one and `ownerSkillId` names the owning skill. A typed `/speckit:save this session` renders `system-spec-kit → /speckit:save (start)`.\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 68]","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":true},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md, lines 28-67\n28: \n29: ---\n30: \n31: ## 2. WHAT IT DOES\n32: \n33: On each user-prompt event the adapter:\n34: \n35: 1. **Kill-switch.** Checks `isHookEnabled('skill-advisor')` (master `SYSTEM_HOOKS_DISABLED` or the `skill-advisor` family). Disabled → empty/skipped prompt-safe output.\n36: 2. **Read the prompt + workspace.** Resolves the workspace root (install-anchored walk, not CWD-relative, so the hook stays correct off-root), reads the prompt text (bounded), and resolves the session id.\n37: 3. **Prompt gate.** `skippedAdvisorResultFor` uses `shouldFireAdvisor` to decline skip commands, short acknowledgements and prompts below its length threshold. A declined prompt returns a skipped result and does not spawn the CLI.\n38: 4. **CLI front door.** The hook calls `buildSkillAdvisorBriefFromCli`, which runs `skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json - --no-warm-only` within the `SPECKIT_CLAUDE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS` budget (2,500 ms by default). It writes the request to the CLI's stdin, so the prompt never appears in the process table. The request carries at most the prompt's first 10,000 characters, the most `advisor_recommend` accepts, so a longer prompt is routed on its head instead of failing open. The Claude shim sets the child budget to 2,200 ms when the operator has not set the variable. An operator value above about 2,200 ms lets the shim kill the hook at 2,500 ms first, so the turn gets `{}`. The CLI owns the warm-daemon probe, starts the daemon when the socket is cold and falls back to the local Python scorer with a `degraded` result. The request carries `includeCompiledRoute: true`, because the advisor derives a recommendation's slash command from the compiled route. It also carries `compiledRouteTopOnly: true`, because each route lookup is a subprocess inside the hook's budget and the brief leads with the top pick. The hook keeps the `command` field and reads nothing from the route itself. A daemon still running an older build rejects these options with JSON-RPC `-32602`, and the CLI retries once without them.\n39: 5. **Render.** `renderAdvisorBrief` produces the model-visible `Advisor:` text. When there is no brief, the handler emits `renderAdvisorFallbackDirective`: one status line, then the directives block. The line reads `Advisor: outage (<status>)` with the command to route by hand when the advisor failed or timed out, `Advisor: no skill matched.` when it answered with nothing above threshold, and `Advisor: prompt skipped.` when the prompt gate declined. The `/doctor:setup` hint appears on no-match and outage lines for prompts that use set up, set-up or setup wording, install or reinstall wording including installation and installer, configure or configuration wording or `check` followed within three words by environment, env, config, hooks, tools, tooling, toolchain or skilled. The no-match line ends with ` For setup or health checks, run /doctor:setup.` The outage line places `for setup or health checks, run /doctor:setup;` before the hand-route command so it stays last. Raw prompt text is never persisted in diagnostics, cache metadata, status, or attribution.\n40: 6. **Directive-lifecycle dedup.** `decideDirectiveLifecycleDelivery` suppresses a re-delivery of the same directive to the same session within its cadence, so a repeated prompt does not re-inject an identical brief. A repeat in a known session keeps the first line, a route or a fallback status, and drops the directives block.\n41: 7. **Emit.** Native adapters emit a `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext` JSON envelope. OpenCode appends to `output.system` via `experimental.chat.system.transform`. Pi sends a model-visible message.\n42: \n43: The injected brief begins with `Advisor:` and names the routed skill or, when the recommendation resolves to a command, the skill that owns it and the runnable command. A doctor command appears as its runnable `/doctor:` string on the top line or under a skill as an `Also:` second line. The handler returns `{}` when the hook is disabled, input cannot be parsed or an unhandled exception occurs. A shim also returns `{}` when it cannot resolve or relay valid child output, including on timeout. When the handler has no brief, it emits the directives fallback.\n44: \n45: ### Brief head form\n46: \n47: A recommendation that resolves to a command renders one label, shared by the `use` and `ambiguous` heads:\n48: \n49: ```text\n50: <ownerSkillId or skill> → <command> (<policy>)\n51: ```\n52: \n53: The recommendation's own `ownerSkillId`, `command` and `policy` are authoritative, and the generated command row fills only what they omit. The policy parenthesis is dropped when no policy is known. A recommendation with no command row keeps its skill label rather than a command label.\n54: \n55: The `use` head reads `Advisor: <freshness>; use <label> <confidence>/<uncertainty> pass.` For example:\n56: \n57: ```text\n58: Advisor: live; use system-spec-kit → /speckit:save (start) 0.89/0.12 pass.\n59: ```\n60: \n61: The ambiguous head applies the same label to both sides: `Advisor: <freshness>; ambiguous: <label> <confidence>/<uncertainty> vs <label> <confidence>/<uncertainty> pass.` Each label part passes `sanitizeSkillLabel`, so a control character, a line break or an instruction-shaped label renders nothing rather than reaching the model. The `Also:` line still names a doctor command by its runnable string.\n62: \n63: ### Degraded contract\n64: \n65: When the daemon is unreachable, the CLI runs the local Python scorer and returns `status: ok` with `degraded: true` and `source: local-scorer`. The local scorer returns command row ids, which a reader cannot type, so the CLI rewrites each row before the brief renders:\n66: \n67: - A row whose bare slash command appears in the prompt (the row's `command` text before the first space) keeps its command fields: `skillId` is the row id, `kind` is `command`, `command` is the runnable string, `policy` is present when the row has one and `ownerSkillId` names the owning skill. A typed `/speckit:save this session` renders `system-spec-kit → /speckit:save (start)`.\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 68]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md\",\"offset\":28,\"limit\":40}"},"time":{"start":1794532659574,"end":1794532660188}}}}
